# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for rcc
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* The back end

The direction is taken: c1 is rcc's only back end, it writes x86-64
assembly itself, and the system cc preprocesses, assembles and
links. The reasons are =docs/findings.md= and =docs/llvm.md=, the
design is =docs/backend.md= with its calls made in
=docs/design-calls.md=. The chapter holds the measurement to redo,
the records of the design to correct, c1's own changes and defects,
and the second target.

** TODO Redo the measurement

=make bench= under c1. Compiled with c1, =collatz-tuned= warns
twice, for =x= and =steps=, and =matmul-tuned= five times, for =pc=,
=bcol=, =pa=, =pb= and =s=; those seven take slots. =sieve-tuned=,
=fib-tuned=, =sort-tuned= and =strings-tuned= warn not at all: sieve
declares six, but its two loops are sibling blocks, so never more than
five are live at once. The numbers move. The movement is a result about
the model, and the tuned sources may be re-tuned to the set, as a PDP-11
programmer tuned to three.

Check: chapter 4 of =cost.md= re-recorded, with the register count
per program, and its opening sentence on the LLVM-era numbers
gone.

** TODO The sixth register's price is counted by declarations

=design-calls.md= 2.2 prices a sixth register from the =register=
variables each tuned =main= declares. But c0 counts the variables
live at once: a block's variables leave the count at its end. The
warnings follow the live count, so sieve, which declares six and
holds at most five live, gets none. Three sentences thus rest
on the wrong count:

- 2.2: "with five, sieve loses one too", where sieve loses none;
- 3.2: "four of six =main= functions exceed five", where two do by
  the live count, matmul and collatz, and three by declarations;
- 2.2, 2.3 and =backend.md= 13.1: the sixth register "was worth one
  variable in one program of six". By the live count it keeps one
  more variable out of a slot in each of matmul and collatz, and
  fits no program whole that five do not.

Fix: 2.2's table gains the most live at once per function, and the
sentences that price the sixth register are restated from it; 2.3's
conclusion is re-read against the restated price.

Check: no sentence of the two documents counts a sixth register
variable by declarations where the rule counts the live ones.

** TODO =design-calls.md= calls the record of the register extent future

=design-calls.md= 3.2 says the five-register extent "can be written
in the cost model, and it will be", and chapter 7 lists "the
implementation-defined extent of =register= recorded as five and the
warning" among the changes "when it is rewritten from the new pins".
=cost.md= chapter 1 now records the extent, as 6.7.2p12 leaves it to
the implementation and 4p10 asks, so both sentences describe as
pending what is done.

Fix: the two sentences say the cost model records the extent, and
point at =cost.md= chapter 1.

Check: no sentence of =design-calls.md= calls the record of the
extent future.

** TODO A memory-form shift on a slot moves its count from =eax=

The effect context's =x <<= n;= on a slot evaluates the count by
=rvalue()=, which takes the lowest free register, =eax=, and
=shift_on()= then moves it to =ecx=: =movl -8(%rbp), %eax; movl %eax,
%ecx; shlb %cl, -9(%rbp)=. Through a pointer the base holds =rax=, so
the count lands in =ecx= at once. The =mov= is a real move of a real
value, but one the generator made for itself.

Fix: =shift_on()= evaluates a count that is not a constant with
=rax= held out of =take()=, so that the count's load takes =rcx= when
it is free, the next register of chapter 4's order.

Check: the probe =int f(int a, int n) { char x = a; x <<= n; return
x; }= has no =movl %eax, %ecx=; the 51 cost pins are unchanged.

** TODO A cast to void and a void =?:= arm leave the effect context

=(void) (i = 0);= and =c ? (i = 1) : (i = 2);= as statements store
each constant through a register, =movl $0, %eax; movl %eax,
-8(%rbp)= in the probe below. =cast()= to void and =conditional()=
of void type evaluate their operands by =rvalue()= and release them,
so the constant assignment inside never reaches =effect()=.

Fix: =cast()= to void and the arms of a void =conditional()= call
=effect()= on their operands.

Check: the probe =void g(int c) { int i; (void) (i = 0); c ? (i = 1)
: (i = 2); }= stores each constant in one instruction; no pin
changes, since none holds either form.

** TODO A shift's reloaded left operand lands in =rcx=

In a variable shift whose left operand is reloaded after a call,
=int c(int); long w(long a, int n) { return a << c(n); }=,
=operands()= reloads the left operand avoiding only the count's
register, so it lands in =rcx=, and =shift()= then moves it straight
out again for the count: =movq -24(%rbp), %rcx; movq %rcx, %rdx=
before =shlq %cl, %rdx=. The =mov= is a real move of a real value,
but one the generator made for itself.

Fix: for a shift, the left operand's reload also avoids =rcx=.

Check: the probe loses the =movq %rcx, %rdx=, and the 51 cost pins
are unchanged.

** TODO aarch64

A second instruction table and the AAPCS64 convention: =x0= to =x7=
for arguments, =x19= to =x28= callee-saved, ten register variables.
After x86-64 is whole.

* Guarantees

The promises in =docs/guarantees.md=. The entry number is the
guarantee it belongs to. All are decided; G15 is withdrawn with its
number reserved. c1 writes none of the guard of G4, the round trip
of G8 and the laundering of G16, which draft 1 designed against an
optimizer, because nothing between the source and the instruction
reasons about the program.

** TODO G7's "Lost" in the rationale misses the equality fold

=rationale.md= says G7 loses "nothing beyond G6". The corpus says
otherwise: =clang -O2= folds =&a + 1 == &b= to false for two distinct
locals, the one-past-the-end address included, where rcc compares the
addresses and finds them equal (=tests/corpus/g7-ptr-equal.c=, 1
under rcc, 0 under clang). A relational compare of the two is not
folded.

Fix: the Lost clause of G7 names the equality fold between distinct
objects, with the corpus program as its probe.

Check: the rationale's G7 names the fold and the program.

** TODO The rationale has no G17

=guarantees.md= has G17, the writable string literal; =rationale.md=
has neither a G17 entry nor its row in the never-emitted table, so
the promise has no recorded argument, alternatives or probe. The corpus
has the probe: =clang -O2= folds the load from a literal to its
initializer and drops the store (=tests/corpus/g17-string-store.c=, 122
under rcc, 97 under clang).

Fix: a Storage section in the rationale with G17: the ISO paragraph,
the promise, the IR, what is lost (equal literals merged, the
read-only section, the fold of a load from a literal), and the probe.

Check: the rationale has a G17 entry and the table row.

* Cost model

The record in =docs/cost.md=: what each construct compiles to under
c1, its table written from the pins of =tests/cost/=. The findings of
the measurement (=docs/findings.md=) were facts about LLVM's code
generator and left with it; that page's chapter 8 says what became
of each. The entries below are rows the table lacks.

** TODO No row pins a multiply or an unsigned division by a power of two

c1 writes =x * 4= as =imull $4, %eax, %eax=, and an unsigned =x / 4=
and =x % 4= as =movl $4, %ecx; xorl %edx, %edx; divl %ecx=, the
remainder then moved from =edx= (=backend.md= 6.4). This is the
thesis's answer to finding 2 of =findings.md=, the selector's
strength reduction. But no cost program pins the three: =mul-three=
multiplies by 3 and =div-four= divides a signed value, so a change to
c1 that turned the three into a shift or a mask would show in no
row.

Fix: a cost program pinning =imull $4=, the =movl $4= into a
register and the =divl= of the unsigned division and remainder.

Check: the row and its pin.

** TODO Chapter 2 has no row for unary minus and complement

Chapter 2 of =cost.md= has no row for =-a= or =~a=. A negative
constant is not one of them: the parser folds =-1= to a constant, so
=long x = -1= is =movq $-1, %rax= and the store, as =long x = 2= is
=movq $2, %rax=, and =p[-1]= is a displacement. On a variable the two
operators are instructions, =negl %eax= and =notl %eax=, the row of
=backend.md= 6.3.

Fix: a row for =-a= and =~a=, with a cost program.

Check: the row and its pin.

* Tests

** TODO The oracle's time limit cannot tell 124 from a timeout

GNU =timeout= exits 124 for a run it stopped and passes the program's
own status otherwise, so a program that exits 124 by itself is
recorded as =timeout=. And =timeout= sends SIGTERM, which a program
may ignore; =timeout -k= would follow with SIGKILL.

Fix: =timeout -k 1 10=, and a corpus rule that no program exits 124.

Check: a program that ignores SIGTERM ends its row.

** TODO A signal death records as 128 + n, like an exit with 128 + n

A program killed by signal n is recorded as 128 + n: =timeout= dies
of the child's signal, and the shell reports 128 + n. So the G9
program's SIGSEGV is the row's 139, and a program that exits 139 by
itself records the same.

Fix: show a status above 128 by its signal name, =SEGV= from =kill
-l=, in the table and in =expected=, and a corpus rule that no
program exits above 128.

Check: the =g9-null-load= row reads =SEGV= and passes.

** TODO A signal death leaves a core file in /tmp

The oracle runs each program with the caller's core limit. Where it is
unlimited and =core_pattern= is =/tmp/core.%e.%p.%s=, as on this
machine, every run of =g9-null-load= under rcc leaves a core of about
290 KB in =/tmp=, and =timeout= says "the monitored command dumped
core" in the log.

Fix: =ulimit -c 0= in the script before the runs.

Check: =make test= leaves no new =/tmp/core.*=.

** TODO A G14 corpus program needs a non-constant condition

=for (;;);= hangs under =clang -O2= as well: C11 6.8.5p6 exempts a
loop whose controlling expression is constant from the
forward-progress assumption. The divergence G14 promises shows only
on a loop like =while (x);= with =x= a non-volatile object the loop
never writes.

Fix: write that program as the G14 entry, expected =timeout= under
rcc and 0 under clang.

Check: the oracle row.

** TODO The register rows pin only part of c1's register rules

The five programs that declare =register= pin the registers in
declaration order, the pushes with their unwind offsets, a register
parameter's move from its argument register, a read as an operand, a
copy before an instruction that writes, an assignment, a constant's
immediate, and G5's unwritten register. No program of either corpus
shows the rest of =backend.md= chapter 4, so a change to any of these
shows in no pin:

- a compound assignment or a =++= on the register in place, =addl $1,
  %r13d=;
- a narrow register variable: the extending store =movsbl %al,
  %ebx=, the re-extension after a step, and an unwritten one's
  extension at its declaration;
- the copy of a held read before a write, =i + i++=, and before the
  paths of =&&=, =||= and =?:= (G13);
- a register parameter past the sixth, loaded from its stack slot,
  and the order of the moves and the stores among mixed parameters;
- the epilogue's =leaq -8k(%rbp), %rsp= before the pops, which only a
  function with pushes and a =subq= writes;
- the sixth live variable in a slot, and a later block taking =rbx=
  again.

Fix: a cost program for each, with its pin.

Check: perturbing each rule in =c1.cc= turns a row red.

** TODO =-o -= names a file, not standard output

=rcc -S -o -= writes a file called =-= in the working directory. Every
cc reads =-o -= as standard output for =-S=, and a reader of the
assembly expects it.

Fix: with =-S=, an output of =-= writes the text to standard output;
with =-c= or a link it stays a usage error.

Check: =rcc -S -o - x.c | head -1= prints the first line of the
assembly and leaves no file.

* Parser

** TODO A warning recorded before an error is never printed

The parser records a warning in the unit, and the driver prints the
unit's warnings only after a successful parse. =error_t= ends the
parse at the first error, so the unit and its warnings are lost with
the exception. Thus =&k= on a sixth register variable reports the
error alone: the warning that put =k= in a slot was recorded at its
declaration, before the error, and is never printed.

Fix: the driver prints the warnings the parser recorded before the
error too. Since the unit is lost with the exception, the warnings
need a home the exception does not destroy, such as a list the
driver passes to the parser.

Check: =int f(void) { register int a, b, c, d, e, k; return *&k; }=
prints the warning at =k= and then the error at =&=, exit status 1.

** TODO Assignment to a const object is not diagnosed

The parser accepts =const= and drops it: the type is the machine's
view and carries no qualifier. So =const int c = 1; c = 2;= compiles,
though 6.5.17.1p2 requires a modifiable lvalue and 6.3.2.1p1 excludes
a const-qualified one; a conforming compiler must diagnose it. The
same silence covers a =const= pointee assigned through, and a
=T *= initialized from a =const T *=.

Fix: qualifiers on the symbol and on the pointee, checked by
=require_modifiable_lvalue= and by the assignment conversion; the
type itself stays unqualified.

Check: the three programs above fail with a diagnostic; =const= in
every other position still parses.

** TODO GNU extensions in system headers reach the parser as identifiers

`clang -E` of a program that includes `stdio.h` or most other glibc
headers leaves `__attribute__`, `__extension__`, `__restrict`,
`__inline`, `__asm__` and their arguments in the text; the lexer
hands them over as identifiers and the parser refuses the first.
`limits.h` has none, so the first slice does not meet them. Any
corpus program that calls into libc will. The same family: `_Bool`,
`bool`, `inline` and every keyword outside =tok_t= arrive as
identifiers and fail as undeclared names rather than as `not yet`.

Fix: decide between skipping the extensions in the parser (an
attribute and its parenthesized argument list dropped, the others
dropped as words) and preprocessing with a flag set that hides them.
A probe says which headers need what.

Check: `#include <stdio.h>` followed by an empty `main` parses.

** TODO A parameter's array size cannot name an earlier parameter

=int f(int n, int a[n])= fails with "'n' undeclared". Before the
evaluator, any size but one integer token was a not-yet; the size is
now parsed as an expression, and the name in it is looked up. The
parser declares a definition's parameters only after the whole
declarator, in =function_definition=, and a prototype's never, so no
parameter is in scope inside the list. The program is valid C: =n='s
scope begins just after its declarator (6.2.1p7), and a definition's
parameter list has block scope (6.2.1p4). The size changes nothing:
6.7.7.4p6 adjusts the parameter to a pointer, and at prototype scope
6.7.7.3p5 treats a size that is not an integer constant expression as
=*=.

Fix: a scope opened at the parameter list, each parameter declared as
its declarator completes, which a definition keeps as its body's
scope; a parameter's size that is not an integer constant expression
taken as =*=. In a definition this skips the evaluation of the size
that 6.7.7.3p5 asks for, which only a size with a side effect shows.

Check: =int f(int n, int a[n]) { return a[0]; }= compiles to the
listing of =int f(int n, int *a)=; =int g(int n) { int a[n]; }= is
still the variable-length not-yet.

** TODO A =(void *) 0= arm makes =?:= a =void *=

=c ? (void *) 0 : p= with =p= an =int *= has type =void *= in the
tree, so =*(c ? (void *) 0 : p)= fails with "indirection through a
pointer to void". 6.5.16p7 gives the result the type of the other
operand when one operand is a null pointer constant, and =(void *) 0=
is one (6.3.2.3p3); the result is an =int *=. =common_pointer()=
asks whether an operand is a null pointer constant only when it is
not a pointer. Two pointers of different types thus meet at =void *=
whenever one is a =void *=, which is p7's rule for a pointer to void
that is not a null pointer constant.

Fix: in =common_pointer()=, a null pointer constant takes the other
operand's type before the rule for =void *= applies.

Check: =int f(int c, int *p) { return *(c ? (void *) 0 : p); }=
compiles; =c ? v : p= with =v= a =void *= variable is still =void *=.

** TODO The operand of =!= is not promoted

=!E= is defined as =(0==E)= (6.5.4.3p5), and the equality operator
promotes a =char= operand to =int=. The parser builds =!c= on the
unpromoted =char=, and c1 compares it at the =char='s width, =cmpb
$0=. The result is the same, 0 or 1, but the tree does not say what
the standard says, and the listing shows a byte compare where the
operation is on an =int=.

Fix: the parser inserts the promotion cast on the operand of =!=; c1
then writes =cmpl=.

Check: the listing of =!c= on a =char= is the load =movsbl= and
=cmpl $0=.

* Lexer

